      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\lfloor \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor x + 2 \\rfloor\\), we need to analyze the behavior of the floor function.\n\nLet's start by setting \\( y = \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor - \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} < \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor \\leq \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite \\( \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor \\) as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} < 2x \\leq \\frac{3}{2} \\implies 0 < x \\leq \\frac{5}{4}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's consider \\(\\lfloor x \\rfloor\\). We analyze the inequality \\(\\lfloor x + 2 \\rfloor\\):\n\\[\nx + 2 \\geq \\lfloor x + 2 \\rfloor \\geq x\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to determine when these two expressions are equal:\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor x + 2 \\rfloor\n\\]\n\nConsider different values of \\( x \\) within the interval \\( 0 < x \\leq \\frac{5}{4} \\).\n\n1. **Case 1: \\( 0 < x < \\frac{5}{4} \\)**\n\n- When \\( x = 1 \\):\n  \\[\n  \\lfloor 2 \\cdot 1 \\rfloor = \\lfloor 2 \\cdot 1 \\rfloor = \\lfloor 2 \\rfloor = 2\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\lfloor \\lfloor 2 \\cdot 1 \\rfloor - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 2 - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 1.5 \\rfloor = 1\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\lfloor 1 + 2 \\rfloor = \\lfloor 3 \\rfloor = 3\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  1 \\neq 3\n  \\]\n\n- When \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n  \\[\n  \\lfloor 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor = 1\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\lfloor \\lfloor 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 0.5 \\rfloor = 0\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\lfloor \\frac{1}{2} + 2 \\rfloor = \\lfloor \\frac{5}{2} \\rfloor = 2\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  0 \\neq 2\n  \\]\n\nGiven these examples when \\( x \\) is not within the interval \\( (0, 1.5) \\):\n\n2. **General Inspection**:\n   As \\( x \\) moves within \\((0, 1.5)\\), we know:\n   \\[\n   \\lfloor \\lfloor 2x \\rfloor - \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor\n   \\]\n   consistently maps to intervals that disregard likelihood of equating bids. Hence, there will not be equality unless considering intervals denoting \\(\\lfloor x + 2 \\rfloor = k\\).\n\n**Conclusion**: The most straightforward analysis aligns constant rankings most correctly form specific floor intervals \\( \\lfloor x + 2 \\rfloor = k \\):\n\\[\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n\\]\nExclusion additional marginal overestimation \n\nThe boxed solution is:\n\\(\\boxed{ 0\\leq x\\leq 1 } \\)",
